From: Ubuntu iceland port <jstockdale@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] watchdog: qcom: stop a bootloader-armed watchdog at probe

The OnePlus Pad 4's ABL hands off with the (hypervisor-emulated) APSS
watchdog armed. The existing probe path re-arms it and relies on the
core to keep it fed, but on this platform the deadline still expires
~30 s later, and userspace open/ping/close cycles shorten the fuse
further. Upstream U-Boot took the same decision for Snapdragon
("watchdog: qcom: stop watchdog by default"): stop a running watchdog
at probe; anything that wants one running can open the device and own
it on purpose.

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
index 4eb1bf979012..72993d34a712 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
@@ -340,15 +340,15 @@ static int qcom_wdt_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	watchdog_init_timeout(&wdt->wdd, 0, dev);
 
 	/*
-	 * If WDT is already running, call WDT start which
-	 * will stop the WDT, set timeouts as bootloader
-	 * might use different ones and set running bit
-	 * to inform the WDT subsystem to ping the WDT
+	 * The bootloader can leave the WDT running (measured on the
+	 * OnePlus Pad 4: ABL arms it and the hypervisor-emulated timer
+	 * bites ~30 s after the re-arm below, with userspace pings only
+	 * making it worse). Do what U-Boot does on Snapdragon since
+	 * 2026: stop it outright; userspace that wants a watchdog can
+	 * open and own it deliberately.
 	 */
-	if (qcom_wdt_is_running(&wdt->wdd)) {
-		qcom_wdt_start(&wdt->wdd);
-		set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt->wdd.status);
-	}
+	if (qcom_wdt_is_running(&wdt->wdd))
+		qcom_wdt_stop(&wdt->wdd);
 
 	ret = devm_watchdog_register_device(dev, &wdt->wdd);
 	if (ret)
